16	// THE PACK UPDATE FLOW (docs/versioned-updates/DESIGN.md §3): move one repo's
17	// declared packs from the versions it has installed to the ones this canon ships.
18	// The same shape as the engine flow beside it — vendor, migrate, converge, stamp,
19	// gate — with three deliberate differences, each a consequence of what a pack IS:
20	//
21	//   1. IT PUSHES NO WORKFLOW FILE, because it cannot: GitHub refuses the Action's
22	//      GITHUB_TOKEN under `.github/workflows/` and rejects the WHOLE ref for trying, so
23	//      one such path in the pushed tree fails the entire update. What it does instead is
24	//      the WITHHOLD lane — the write is staged to `.claudinite/pending-workflows/` and
25	//      reported in `withheld`, which raises the apply stage, whose session holds a
26	//      credential that may write there (#1509). The lane was retired in #1317 on the
27	//      premise that a member's workflows are static after adoption; #1494's executor
28	//      line is the counterexample that re-opened it.
29	//   2. IT ENFORCES `minEngineVersion`. A pack version declares the lowest engine it
30	//      runs on; applying it past that is a guess, and a guess about whether a
31	//      member's engine can load a pack is how a fleet goes quiet. Violation is a
32	//      terminal, never a downgrade to "try anyway".
33	//   3. IT HAS AN AGENTIC TAIL — the one place agentic work survives, because the
34	//      pack's new rules meet member-authored content the canon has never seen, and
35	//      because nothing deterministic can run the member's own test suite over what
36	//      the cycle just wrote (./converge-scope.mjs). This module is the
37	//      DETERMINISTIC half and ends by saying whether that stage is needed; the
38	//      stage itself belongs to the shell that can dispatch a session.
39	//
40	// Everything else is shared with the engine flow on purpose: the same version
41	// predicate decides the gap, the same self-test gates the merge, and every
42	// non-green end is the same `needs-human` terminal (DESIGN §5).
43	const canonRoot = dirname(dirname(dirname(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))))); // <canon>/packs/claudinite-lifecycle/updates/<this file>
44	
45	// A pack's own half of the vendor set: its directory, and nothing else. The engine
46	// tree and the catalog belong to the engine flow, which is what keeps two flows
47	// converging one mount from ever fighting over a file.
48	export const isPackFile = (rel, id) => rel.startsWith(`packs/${id}/`);
49	
50	// The mount directories a pack has been vendored under BEFORE — every spelling that
51	// resolves to this id today (renamed-packs.mjs). A rename moves a pack's canon
52	// directory, and the vendor step below replaces a tree per id: the old directory
53	// matches no id any more, so without this it would sit in the mount forever holding a
54	// complete, loadable copy of the pack. That copy is not inert — `discoverPacks`
55	// canonicalizes a mounted pack's own id, so the stale tree announces the SAME id as
56	// the live one and the member runs the pack twice, one of them frozen at whatever it
57	// was renamed from. Swept here rather than by a migration record so the cleanup is a
58	// property of renaming, not something each rename has to remember.
59	export const legacySpellingsOf = (id) =>
60	  Object.entries(RENAMED_PACKS).filter(([, to]) => to === id).map(([from]) => from);
61	
62	// The records this repo still needs for one pack, oldest first — the same predicate
63	// the engine flow ranges over, with that pack's number instead of the engine's.
64	export function packRecordsInGap(id, installed, { today } = {}) {
65	  return migrationDirs().filter((d) => {
66	    const of = flowOf(d);
67	    return of.flow === 'pack' && of.pack === id && migrationApplies(d, { installed, today });
68	  });
69	}
